id: pm-linear-hwol
title: "A globally-scoped preflight override collides with every other installed package, so pm health can never be green"
description: "registerPreflight used the bare-function (global) form, which collides pairwise with every other installed package's preflight override (pm health reports extension_preflight_override_collision). Rewrite it to the scoped object form declaring exactly the mutating linear command paths commandMutatesLinear recognizes (linear sync, linear import, linear export). The runtime matches a command against the commands array by exact normalized path, so the array lists full command paths, not bare top-level names (which would never match linear sync and silently disable the credential gate). The authoritative gate (sentinel option consumed by the handlers) stays in the override body; this only narrows its scope."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-14T06:22:39.772Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T14:59:28.509Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-14T06:23:38.357Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-14T06:23:38.357Z"
author: pi-agent
comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-15T14:13:34.363Z",claude-agent,"Correction to this item's description, recorded as an append-only event rather than a rewrite (CodeRabbit, PR #67).\n\nThe description above names the scoped command paths as \"linear sync, linear import, linear export\" and refers to the classifier as `commandMutatesLinear`. Both are now out of date:\n\n1. **The scope is four paths, not three.** `linear-sync import` — the deprecated importer alias — reaches the same write path as `linear import`. Under the previous global registration it was covered by accident; narrowing the override to a command list made the omission load-bearing, so it is explicitly in scope. The registered array is `[\"linear sync\", \"linear import\", \"linear export\", \"linear-sync import\"]`.\n\n2. **The classifier is now `commandNeedsLinearAccess`.** Review round 2 showed the mutation question was the wrong one to ask, and got both edges wrong in opposite directions — the preflight guards *credentials and reachability*, not writes:\n   - `linear export --push --dry-run` writes nothing and is documented as making no network call, yet the mutation test saw `--push` and demanded `LINEAR_API_KEY`, so the offline preview failed on any host without a key.\n   - `linear sync|import --atomic --dry-run` also writes nothing but is *not* offline — it fetches issues to build the atomic plan. The mutation test saw `--dry-run` and skipped the preflight, so a missing key surfaced as a raw fetch failure inside the sync core instead of the clean USAGE error.\n\n   Renamed and rewritten to answer \"does this invocation reach Linear\", which is what the preflight actually gates.\n\n3. **The scope↔classifier binding is now bidirectional.** The original test walked `override.commands` only, so it could never detect the drift that matters most: a command the classifier requires credentials for while the scope omits it, which silently loses its credential gate entirely. The test now enumerates thirteen invocations with their expected verdict, asserts scope membership for every credential-requiring one, and asserts that every scoped command has a declared expectation.\n\nBoth classifier fixes verified non-vacuous: reverting either edge fails the test. 71/71 pass.\n"
  "2026-08-15T14:59:28.053Z",claude-agent,"CI failure on PR #67, and what it exposed.\n\nThe `test (22)` / `test (26)` jobs failed on `Verify tracked pm project health`, reporting:\n\n```\nhistory_drift_hash_mismatch:pm-linear-hwol\nhistory_drift_chain_mismatch:pm-linear-hwol\n```\n\nNot a corrupt history. The append-only comment recording the description correction was written by pm **2026.8.15** (the CLI on PATH), while this package pinned `@unbrained/pm-cli` as `^2026.8.6` and CI resolved **2026.8.6** — a version that cannot verify the newer hash chain. Reproduced both ways on this host against the same bytes:\n\n- `./node_modules/.bin/pm health` (2026.8.6) → both drift warnings\n- `pm health` (2026.8.15) → no drift\n\nUpstream has this class already (unbraind/pm-cli#976, closed), so the fix belongs on our side: agree on one CLI version.\n\nFixing it turned up that this package was behind the fleet convention in **three** places at once, each stating a host-compatibility fact to a different consumer, and all three stale:\n\n1. `devDependencies` was `^2026.8.6` — a caret range, not a pin. That is precisely how the 2026.8.14 truncated-`list-all` build reached packages with no diff to review, and here it also meant CI could disagree with the history the tracker writes. Now exactly `2026.8.15`.\n2. `peerDependencies` was `>=2026.7.29`, below the floor the fleet standardised on. Now `>=2026.8.15`.\n3. `manifest.json` declared the floor as `pm.pm_min_version` — nested one level below where the host's version gate looks, which reads top-level `pm_min_version`. Verified against the SDK's own `checkExtensionManifestCompatibility`: the manifest returned `compatible: true` for every host tested back to 2026.1.1, including 2026.8.14. **The floor was not merely stale, it was never enforced.** Hoisted to top level; the gate now refuses 2026.8.14 with `pm_min_version_unmet` and still accepts later hosts.\n\nThis is the third distinct spelling of the same unenforced-floor defect found across the fleet today — pm-context used `compatibility: { pm: ... }`, pm-beads had the right field with a stale value, pm-linear nested it under `pm`. All three packages' suites were fully green throughout. Reported upstream as unbraind/pm-cli#1032, asking for an advisory finding when a manifest declares no enforceable bound and for unrecognized manifest keys to be reported.\n\nTwo regression tests added, matching the ones now in pm-beads and pm-context: one binds `manifest.json` `pm_min_version` to the package.json peer floor and asserts the dev pin is exact rather than a range; the other runs the SDK host gate against the manifest bytes on disk so a floor moved back under a nested key fails loudly instead of shipping inert.\n\nVerified after the change: `pm health` under the pinned 2026.8.15 reports no drift and `--strict-exit` returns 0. `release:check` green, 71/71 tests.\n"
files[5]{path,scope}:
